+ Troubleshooting & Best Practices', '{"Day": "Day 15", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "Async Programming, Promises, Fetch API\n+ Troubleshooting & Best Practices", "Module": "Foundation", "Sub Topic": "Synchronous vs asynchronous execution and the JavaScript event-loop model at an application level\nCreating, chaining and composing Promises\nasync/await syntax, try/catch/finally and error propagation\nFetch API for GET/POST/PUT/DELETE style REST calls\nRequest headers, query parameters, JSON payloads and response parsing\nHTTP status handling and conversion of failures into user/application states\nSequential vs parallel asynchronous operations using Promise utilities\nLoading, success, empty and failure-state handling for API-driven screens\nTroubleshooting & Best Practices – JavaScript / TypeScript & Async:\n• Debugging with browser DevTools: breakpoints, call stack, console and Network tab inspection\n• Troubleshooting common async defects: missing await, unhandled promise rejections, race conditions and stale responses\n• Resolving frequent TypeScript type/compile errors and Fetch failures (CORS, 4xx/5xx, JSON parse errors)\n• Best practices: consistent error handling, typed API responses, request timeouts/AbortController, ESLint/Prettier conventions\nDelivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, integration or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Async Programming, Promises, Fetch API\nInput: PulseCart foundation requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Consume the supplied product and account endpoints; show loading, success and failure states using Fetch.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Async Programming, Promises, Fetch API increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Implement reliable asynchronous API consumption with clear success and failure handling.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(106, 4, 27, 'Day 16', 'Components, Props, State, Hooks', '{"Day": "Day 16", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "Components, Props, State, Hooks", "Module": "React", "Sub Topic": "React application structure, JSX and component composition\nFunctional components and reusable UI boundaries\nProps, event callbacks and parent-child data flow\nLocal component state with useState\nSide effects and lifecycle-style behavior with useEffect\nControlled UI behavior and derived state\nState lifting, state colocation and reusable component composition\nIntroduction to custom hooks for reusable stateful behavior Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, integration or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Components, Props, State, Hooks\nInput: PulseCart React web requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Convert the catalog shell into reusable React components for product cards, filters, cart badge and account summary.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Components, Props, State, Hooks increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Build reusable React components and manage state using props, state and hooks.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(107, 4, 28, 'Day 17', 'Routing, Forms, Validation', '{"Day": "Day 17", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "Routing, Forms, Validation", "Module": "React", "Sub Topic": "Client-side routing concepts and route configuration\nRoute parameters, navigation links and multi-page user journeys\nNested/page-level component composition for navigable applications\nControlled forms and form-state management\nField-level and form-level validation rules\nValidation messages, submission states and user feedback\nHandling invalid navigation/form states safely\nBuilding a complete routed flow from entry page to transaction/confirmation page", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Routing, Forms, Validation\nInput: PulseCart React web requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Add routes for catalog, product, cart and sign-in; implement a delivery-preference form with validation.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Routing, Forms, Validation increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Build navigable React journeys with validated forms and predictable user feedback.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(149, 5, 37, 'Day 18', 'Chaos Engineering & Failure Testing', '{"Day": "Day 18", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "Chaos Engineering & Failure Testing", "Module": "Resilience Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Form hypotheses, inject controlled failures, observe system behavior, apply guardrails and use results to strengthen resilience. Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, safety or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Controlled Failure Experiment:\n• Define hypothesis for pod/service dependency failure.\n• Establish abort conditions and blast-radius controls.\n• Inject failure in test environment.\n• Observe alerts, recovery and customer impact.\n• Record resilience gaps.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Chaos experiment plan + execution evidence + resilience-gap backlog.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(108, 4, 29, 'Day 18', 'State Management (Context / Redux Toolkit)', '{"Day": "Day 18", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "State Management (Context / Redux Toolkit)", "Module": "React", "Sub Topic": "When local state is enough vs when shared application state is required\nReact Context for lightweight cross-component state\nRedux Toolkit store, slices, actions and reducers\nSelectors and predictable read access to shared state\nUpdating normalized/shared state without uncontrolled mutation\nManaging session, cart and preference state across multiple screens\nAsync-aware state transitions and loading/error metadata\nChoosing Context vs Redux Toolkit based on application complexity and ownership Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, integration or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – State Management (Context / Redux Toolkit)\nInput: PulseCart React web requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Centralize cart, user session and delivery-preference state so multiple pages remain synchronized.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working State Management (Context / Redux Toolkit) increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Manage cross-page application state predictably using Context or Redux Toolkit.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(109, 4, 30, 'Day 19', 'Building Robust API Integrations', '{"Day": "Day 19", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "Building Robust API Integrations", "Module": "React", "Sub Topic": "Creating a reusable frontend API/service layer\nCalling REST endpoints from React using Fetch-based abstractions\nRequest payload construction and response mapping\nLoading, success, empty and error-state orchestration\nHandling 4xx/5xx responses and user-friendly error messages\nRetry/retry-safe interaction patterns and duplicate-action prevention\nCentralized error normalization for consistent UI handling\nKeeping API contracts aligned with component/state models", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – API Integration, Error Handling\nInput: PulseCart React web requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Connect catalog, authentication and order APIs; surface field, authorization and server errors without breaking journeys.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working API Integration, Error Handling increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Integrate React with backend APIs and implement resilient user-facing error handling.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(110, 4, 31, 'Day 20', 'Performance Optimization, Code Splitting', '{"Day": "Day 20", "Week": "Week 5", "Topic": "Performance Optimization, Code Splitting", "Module": "React", "Sub Topic": "Understanding common React performance bottlenecks\nDetecting unnecessary renders and expensive computations\nReact.memo and component memoization use cases\nuseMemo and useCallback usage with correct dependency management\nLazy loading components/routes with React.lazy and Suspense\nRoute-level and feature-level code splitting\nReducing initial bundle work and deferring low-frequency functionality\nValidating performance improvements without introducing stale state or logic defects Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, integration or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Performance Optimization, Code Splitting\nInput: PulseCart React web requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Lazy-load lower-frequency routes and reduce avoidable re-renders in catalog/cart screens; capture before/after evidence.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Performance Optimization, Code Splitting increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Improve React runtime and loading performance using optimization and code-splitting techniques.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
