	(112, 4, 33, 'Day 22', 'API Integration, Error Handling', '{"Day": "Day 22", "Week": "Week 5", "Topic": "API Integration, Error Handling", "Module": "React", "Sub Topic": "End-to-end REST integration for catalog, sign-in/account and order-related flows\nAuthentication token propagation from the web client to protected APIs\nCentralized request headers and response handling\nLoading, empty, validation, unauthorized and server-error states\nAPI contract validation against expected request/response shapes\nError normalization and reusable user-facing error presentation\nHandling token expiry/authorization failures without corrupting UI state\nIntegration troubleshooting using browser network tools and API-test evidence", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Backend Integration Hardening\nInput: PulseCart React web journey plus catalog, authentication and order REST contracts.\nImplement: Add reusable request handling, token propagation, loading/error states and response validation.\nIntegrate: Connect the web journey to protected and unprotected backend endpoints using a consistent API layer.\nValidate: Test success, validation failure, unauthorized, not-found and server-error scenarios; verify request/response shapes.\nOutput: Production-style React-to-backend integration with predictable error and loading behavior.\nEvidence: Source code, browser network traces, API-test evidence and short troubleshooting note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Integrate backend APIs, authentication/token handling, API-contract checks and robust loading/error behavior within the normal hands-on flow.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(113, 4, 37, 'Day 23', 'Components, Navigation', '{"Day": "Day 23", "Week": "Week 5", "Topic": "Components, Navigation", "Module": "React Native", "Sub Topic": "React Native project structure and component fundamentals\nCore mobile components and reusable screen composition\nStyling approaches for native layouts\nNavigation container, stack/tab patterns and screen registration\nRoute parameters and navigation between key user journeys\nManaging screen-level state and user interactions\nReusable mobile UI components for catalog, account and order flows\nBuilding navigation that remains predictable across multiple screens Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, integration or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Components, Navigation\nInput: PulseCart React Native requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Create mobile catalog, product, cart and account screens with a coherent navigation hierarchy.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Components, Navigation increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Build reusable React Native screens and navigation for key mobile journeys.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(114, 4, 38, 'Day 24', 'API Integration', '{"Day": "Day 24", "Week": "Week 5", "Topic": "API Integration", "Module": "React Native", "Sub Topic": "Calling REST APIs from React Native using the same backend contracts as the web application\nShared/mobile-specific API service organization\nRequest lifecycle: trigger, loading, success, empty and failure states\nParsing and mapping API responses into mobile view models\nHandling offline/slow-network style failures gracefully\nAuthentication-aware request construction\nAvoiding duplicate requests during screen transitions/re-renders\nValidating consistency between React Web and React Native API behavior", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – API Integration\nInput: PulseCart React Native requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Connect mobile catalog, authentication and order-status screens to the same PulseCart APIs used by the web app.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working API Integration increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Integrate React Native screens with backend APIs and handle request lifecycle states.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(115, 4, 39, 'Day 25', 'Local Storage, AsyncStorage', '{"Day": "Day 25", "Week": "Week 6", "Topic": "Local Storage, AsyncStorage", "Module": "React Native", "Sub Topic": "Purpose and limitations of local mobile storage\nAsyncStorage read, write, update and remove operations\nSerialization/deserialization of application preferences\nHydrating state when the application starts\nSeparating cacheable preferences from sensitive data\nHandling missing, stale or malformed stored values\nCoordinating stored preferences with server-fetched data\nReset/logout behavior and local-state cleanup Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, integration or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Local Storage, AsyncStorage\nInput: PulseCart React Native requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Persist delivery display preferences and selected non-sensitive app settings, then restore them on relaunch.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Local Storage, AsyncStorage increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Persist and restore appropriate mobile state using AsyncStorage.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(137, 5, 15, 'Day 6', 'Terraform / CDK (IaC), Containers & Kubernetes', '{"Day": "Day 6", "Week": "Week 2", "Topic": "Terraform / CDK (IaC), Containers & Kubernetes", "Module": "Cloud & Infrastructure", "Sub Topic": "Model repeatable infrastructure and containerized service deployment; connect IaC structure to Kubernetes workload reliability and deployment concerns. Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, safety or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Lab 3 – Infrastructure as Code, Part B:\n• Create Terraform modules for VPC/network, ALB, compute/EKS-facing resources and RDS dependencies.\n• Package the Order service as a container.\n• Define Kubernetes Deployment/Service manifests and health probes.\n• Run plan/validation before apply.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Terraform module baseline + container artifact + Kubernetes manifests.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(116, 4, 40, 'Day 26', 'App Lifecycle, Permissions', '{"Day": "Day 26", "Week": "Week 6", "Topic": "App Lifecycle, Permissions", "Module": "React Native", "Sub Topic": "React Native application lifecycle and foreground/background transitions\nUsing lifecycle signals to refresh time-sensitive application data\nPermission request, grant, denial and fallback flows\nPermission-aware UI behavior and user messaging\nAvoiding unnecessary background work\nRecovering application state after resume\nCoordinating lifecycle events with API refresh and local storage\nTesting lifecycle and permission edge cases on target platforms", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – App Lifecycle, Permissions\nInput: PulseCart React Native requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Refresh order status when the app returns to foreground and implement a permission-safe device-feature interaction.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working App Lifecycle, Permissions increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Handle app lifecycle changes and permissions without disrupting user journeys.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(117, 4, 41, 'Day 27', 'Platform Considerations (iOS / Android)', '{"Day": "Day 27", "Week": "Week 6", "Topic": "Platform Considerations (iOS / Android)", "Module": "React Native", "Sub Topic": "iOS vs Android platform behavior that affects React Native applications\nPlatform-specific layout and interaction differences\nSafe-area, keyboard and device-size considerations\nConditional platform logic using supported platform mechanisms\nPermission and device-feature differences across platforms\nValidating common journeys on both platforms\nKeeping shared code dominant while isolating justified platform-specific behavior\nDocumenting and testing cross-platform deviations before release Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, integration or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Platform Considerations (iOS / Android)\nInput: PulseCart React Native requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Review and adapt one shared journey for platform-specific behavior, then document iOS/Android differences.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Platform Considerations (iOS / Android) increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Design and validate cross-platform behavior for iOS and Android.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(118, 4, 42, 'Day 28', 'End-to-End Mobile App Development & Troubleshooting', '{"Day": "Day 28", "Week": "Week 6", "Topic": "End-to-End Mobile App Development & Troubleshooting", "Module": "React Native", "Sub Topic": "Assembling the complete mobile journey using reusable screens and navigation\nScreen-to-screen state transfer and route parameter handling\nIntegrating API-driven content into catalog/account/order screens\nReusing AsyncStorage for non-sensitive preferences and session-adjacent state\nLifecycle-aware refresh when the app returns to foreground\nPermission-aware device interaction within the user journey\nCross-platform verification of the same business flow on iOS and Android\nEnd-to-end mobile troubleshooting using logs, network evidence and reproducible test steps\nTroubleshooting & Best Practices – React Native:\n• Debugging with React Native DevTools, Metro bundler logs and device logs (Android Logcat / Xcode console)\n• Troubleshooting common React Native issues: Metro cache/build failures, native dependency linking, Android Gradle and iOS CocoaPods errors, navigation state bugs, platform-specific layout issues\n• Mobile performance troubleshooting: FlatList optimization, avoidable re-renders, image and memory handling\n• Best practices: project structure, platform-specific code (Platform API / .ios/.android files), secure token storage (not AsyncStorage), environment configuration and release-build checks", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – React Native Journey Consolidation\nInput: Existing PulseCart mobile screens, navigation, API services and stored preferences from prior sessions.\nImplement: Assemble the key catalog, product, account and order journeys into one coherent navigation flow.\nIntegrate: Combine API calls, AsyncStorage preferences, lifecycle refresh and permission-aware device behavior within the mobile journey.\nValidate: Exercise navigation/state continuity, loading/error paths and iOS/Android behavior.\nOutput: Integrated React Native journey representing the mobile version of the same PulseCart business capability.\nEvidence: Working build, screenshots/screen recording, logs and short cross-platform validation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Demonstrate an integrated React Native journey with navigation, API integration, local storage, lifecycle/permissions and cross-platform checks embedded into hands-on work.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(119, 4, 46, 'Day 29', 'Backend + Frontend Integration', '{"Day": "Day 29", "Week": "Week 6", "Topic": "Backend + Frontend Integration", "Module": "Full Stack Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Aligning frontend models with existing backend REST contracts\nTracing request/response flow from backend service to React Web and React Native\nDTO/payload mapping and validation at integration boundaries\nConsistent error contracts and client-side interpretation\nHandling version/contract mismatches without silent failures\nCross-origin/environment configuration considerations for web integration\nCoordinating shared endpoints across web and mobile clients\nTroubleshooting end-to-end integration with logs, network traces and API-test evidence", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Backend + Frontend Integration\nInput: PulseCart full stack integration requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Trace an order-status request from existing backend contract through web and mobile clients, including request/response mapping.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Backend + Frontend Integration increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "5", "Learning Outcomes": "Own an end-to-end integration flow across backend contract, web and mobile clients.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(138, 5, 16, 'Day 7', 'Networking, Storage, Security Basics', '{"Day": "Day 7", "Week": "Week 2", "Topic": "Networking, Storage, Security Basics", "Module": "Cloud & Infrastructure", "Sub Topic": "Apply TCP/IP, DNS, HTTP/S, load-balancing, storage choice, IAM/security boundaries and secure network paths to production reliability.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Secure Reliability Path:\n• Validate DNS → ALB → service → RDS traffic path.\n• Define security-group/IAM least-privilege rules.\n• Verify storage durability/recovery assumptions.\n• Simulate one connectivity failure and trace the fault domain.", "Duration (Hrs)": "3.1333", "Learning Outcomes": "Validated service path + security rules + observable Order journey + evidence pack.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(169, 6, 19, NULL, 'Policies (rate limiting, transformation, security)', '{"Topic": "Policies (rate limiting, transformation, security)", "Sub-Category": "Azure API Management (APIM)", "Assessment Area": "Azure"}'),
	(120, 4, 47, 'Day 30', 'Authentication, Authorization (JWT, OAuth)', '{"Day": "Day 30", "Week": "Week 7", "Topic": "Authentication, Authorization (JWT, OAuth)", "Module": "Full Stack Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Authentication vs authorization responsibilities across the full-stack flow\nJWT structure and token-based request flow\nLogin, token receipt, client usage and expiry handling\nProtecting routes/screens and restricting unauthorized operations\nRole/claim-aware authorization decisions at UI integration points\nOAuth concepts and delegated authorization flow at a practical level\nSecure token handling and avoiding accidental exposure\nHandling 401/403 responses consistently across React Web and React Native", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Authentication, Authorization (JWT, OAuth)\nInput: PulseCart full stack integration requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Protect account and order routes/screens, pass tokens correctly and handle expired/unauthorized responses.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Authentication, Authorization (JWT, OAuth) increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "3", "Learning Outcomes": "Implement secure authentication/authorization integration across web and mobile journeys.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(121, 4, 48, 'Day 31', 'Testing (Unit, Integration, E2E basics)', '{"Day": "Day 31", "Week": "Week 7", "Topic": "Testing (Unit, Integration, E2E basics)", "Module": "Full Stack Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Testing pyramid and responsibility across frontend/full-stack layers\nUnit testing reusable functions and state logic\nReact component testing with Jest and React Testing Library\nMocking dependencies and API interactions\nIntegration testing of API-connected components/screens\nBasic end-to-end flow validation for critical user journeys\nPositive, negative, boundary and failure-path coverage\nDesigning tests that are deterministic, maintainable and useful in CI/CD", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Testing (Unit, Integration, E2E basics)\nInput: PulseCart full stack integration requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Create tests for reusable UI logic, API-integrated screens and one critical user flow from sign-in to order status.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Testing (Unit, Integration, E2E basics) increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "3", "Learning Outcomes": "Design and execute layered tests that protect critical full-stack behavior.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(122, 4, 49, 'Day 32', 'CI/CD, Observability, Logging', '{"Day": "Day 32", "Week": "Week 7", "Topic": "CI/CD, Observability, Logging", "Module": "Full Stack Engineering", "Sub Topic": "CI/CD lifecycle from commit to build, test and deploy\nAutomating lint/build/test quality gates\nEnvironment-specific configuration and deployment variables\nPackaging/deploying React Web and coordinating mobile build/release workflow\nStructured client/integration logging for troubleshooting\nCorrelation identifiers and traceable request journeys where applicable\nBasic observability signals for failures and user-impacting issues\nUsing deployment and operational evidence to validate production readiness", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – CI/CD, Observability, Logging\nInput: PulseCart full stack integration requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Define and exercise a pipeline path that runs checks and deploys the web/mobile deliverables; add correlation-friendly logging.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working CI/CD, Observability, Logging increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "5", "Learning Outcomes": "Connect quality gates, deployment automation and observability to the delivery lifecycle.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(123, 4, 50, 'Day 33', 'Secure Coding Practices', '{"Day": "Day 33", "Week": "Week 7", "Topic": "Secure Coding Practices", "Module": "Full Stack Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Input and output validation at client/integration boundaries\nPreventing unsafe rendering and injection-style client vulnerabilities\nSafe handling of tokens, secrets and sensitive application data\nAvoiding insecure local storage of sensitive information\nDependency hygiene and awareness of vulnerable packages\nSecure error handling without exposing internal details\nAuthorization-aware UI behavior and defensive coding\nSecurity review checklist applied to React Web, React Native and integration code Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, integration or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Secure Coding Practices\nInput: PulseCart full stack integration requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Review PulseCart web/mobile code for insecure storage, overexposed errors and unsafe input handling; remediate findings.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working Secure Coding Practices increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Apply secure coding practices across web, mobile and integration boundaries.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(139, 5, 20, 'Day 8', 'Infrastructure as Code (Terraform)', '{"Day": "Day 8", "Week": "Week 2", "Topic": "Infrastructure as Code (Terraform)", "Module": "Automation & IaC", "Sub Topic": "Deepen Terraform structure, state-aware change planning, modularity, repeatability and safe infrastructure change practices.", "Hands-on Labs": "Lab 3 – Infrastructure as Code, Part C:\n• Refactor RetailPulse Terraform into reusable modules.\n• Parameterize environment values.\n• Execute init/validate/plan.\n• Review proposed change for reliability and blast radius before apply.", "Duration (Hrs)": "3.3333", "Learning Outcomes": "Reusable Terraform module set + clean validation/plan output + change-review checklist.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(170, 6, 20, NULL, 'Versioning and revisions', '{"Topic": "Versioning and revisions", "Sub-Category": "Azure API Management (APIM)", "Assessment Area": "Azure"}'),
	(240, 6, 90, NULL, 'Performance optimization (memoization, lazy loading)', '{"Topic": "Performance optimization (memoization, lazy loading)", "Assessment Area": "React JS"}'),
	(124, 4, 51, 'Day 34', 'AI-Assisted Development Tools', '{"Day": "Day 34", "Week": "Week 7", "Topic": "AI-Assisted Development Tools", "Module": "Full Stack Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Using AI-assisted development for code explanation and unfamiliar-code navigation\nGenerating candidate tests and edge cases from existing implementation\nRefactoring suggestions with developer-controlled acceptance\nPrompting with precise technical context and constraints\nVerifying generated code for correctness, security and maintainability\nAvoiding sensitive-data leakage into non-approved AI tools\nRecording accepted/rejected suggestions and engineering rationale\nTreating AI output as an accelerator, not as an unverified source of truth", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – AI-Assisted Development Tools\nInput: PulseCart full stack integration requirements, sample API payloads and existing project baseline.\nImplement: Use a client-approved AI assistant to propose a refactor/test set, verify every change and record accepted/rejected suggestions.\nIntegrate: Reuse the same catalog, account, cart and order-tracking journey components/contracts already built in prior sessions.\nValidate: Run the feature, verify expected success/failure behavior and check the relevant web/mobile/integration path.\nOutput: Working AI-Assisted Development Tools increment integrated into the continuous PulseCart OneJourney solution.\nEvidence: Source code/configuration, screenshots or test output, and a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "5", "Learning Outcomes": "Use AI-assisted development productively while retaining engineering judgment and validation.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(125, 4, 52, 'Day 35', 'Quality Assurance, Troubleshooting & Best Practices', '{"Day": "Day 35", "Week": "Week 8", "Topic": "Quality Assurance, Troubleshooting & Best Practices", "Module": "Full Stack Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Expanded unit and component test coverage using Jest and React Testing Library\nIntegration tests for authenticated API-driven user journeys\nBasic end-to-end validation of the most critical business path\nAccessibility checks for labels, keyboard behavior, focus and readable feedback\nPerformance sanity checks for rendering, loading and repeated network activity\nError-scenario and edge-case testing for empty, invalid, unauthorized and server-failure responses\nRegression evidence and repeatable test execution suitable for CI/CD\nCreation of a consolidated quality evidence pack for final readiness review\nTroubleshooting & Best Practices – Full Stack:\n• End-to-end troubleshooting across web, mobile, API and deployment: request tracing with correlation IDs, logs and network evidence\n• Common full-stack issues: CORS, token expiry / 401 loops, API contract mismatches, environment/config drift, flaky tests and CI/CD pipeline failures\n• Structured root-cause analysis (5 Whys) and defect/incident write-up\n• Best practices: API contract versioning, config & secrets management, logging standards, test-pyramid discipline, PR/code-review and production-readiness checklists", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Quality & Resilience Hardening\nInput: Authenticated PulseCart web/mobile feature and existing automated tests.\nImplement: Expand Jest/RTL unit/component tests, integration checks and one critical E2E-basic flow; add accessibility, performance and edge-case validation.\nIntegrate: Run the quality checks against the same end-to-end feature and connect repeatable checks to the CI/CD workflow.\nValidate: Prove positive, negative, authorization, accessibility, performance and backend-failure scenarios.\nOutput: Consolidated engineering-quality evidence pack for the PulseCart feature.\nEvidence: Test results, accessibility/performance findings, defect fixes and CI/CD execution evidence.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Produce a consolidated quality evidence pack with unit/component, integration, E2E-basic, accessibility, performance and edge-case validation embedded into hands-on work.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(126, 4, 57, 'Day 36', 'Capstone Day 1 – Build: Requirements-to-Working Feature', '{"Day": "Day 36", "Week": "Week 8", "Topic": "Capstone Day 1 – Build: Requirements-to-Working Feature", "Module": "Capstone Project", "Sub Topic": "Clarify the authenticated booking-status and stay-preference user story and its acceptance criteria; define the backend REST contract and request/response payload shapes; build the React Web journey using reusable components, routing, shared state and API integration; build the equivalent React Native journey with navigation, the same backend contract, and appropriate local persistence for preference display settings; apply authentication/authorization so both journeys are protected routes/screens.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Meridian Stays: Authenticated Booking Status & Stay Preferences – Build\nInput: The Meridian Stays capstone build requirements, representative booking-status and stay-preference API payloads, and the existing capstone project baseline (an empty repository/workspace, not PulseCart) — this is a brand-new, independent case study, not a continuation of PulseCart OneJourney.\nImplement: Independently clarify the authenticated booking-status and stay-preference user story; define the backend REST contract; build the React Web journey and the equivalent React Native journey with authenticated access.\nIntegrate: This is the seed of the whole capstone — nothing to reuse from PulseCart; every later day traces back to the decisions made here.\nValidate: Run the feature end to end on both channels, verify success/failure behavior and confirm web/mobile parity.\nOutput: Working, authenticated booking-status & stay-preference feature for Meridian Stays, built independently.\nEvidence: Backend contract, React Web journey and React Native journey; screenshots or test output; a short implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Independently create an integrated, authenticated cross-channel feature — booking status & stay preferences — spanning backend contract, React Web and React Native, on a platform never seen before.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "Capstone"}'),
	(127, 4, 58, 'Day 37', 'Capstone Day 2 – Automate & Test: Production-Readiness Hardening', '{"Day": "Day 37", "Week": "Week 8", "Topic": "Capstone Day 2 – Automate & Test: Production-Readiness Hardening", "Module": "Capstone Project", "Sub Topic": "Add unit/component/integration/E2E-basic tests for critical paths; validate negative, unauthorized and backend-failure scenarios; apply secure coding and safe token/data-handling practices; review accessibility and performance basics; connect build/test checks to the CI/CD path; add logging/observability evidence for troubleshooting.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Meridian Stays: Authenticated Booking Status & Stay Preferences – Production Readiness\nInput: The Day 24 Meridian Stays feature (backend contract, React Web journey, React Native journey), plus the capstone hardening and quality requirements.\nImplement: Add unit/component/integration/E2E-basic tests, secure handling, failure scenarios, logging/observability evidence and CI/CD wiring for the capstone feature.\nIntegrate: Every test, security fix and pipeline check targets the exact feature built independently in Day 24 — not a hardening exercise on sample or PulseCart code.\nValidate: Confirm positive, negative, unauthorized and backend-failure scenarios all pass; confirm the CI/CD path runs the new checks.\nOutput: Layered test suite, secure-coding review and CI/CD-connected quality evidence for the Meridian Stays feature.\nEvidence: Test results, secure-coding review notes, accessibility/performance findings, CI/CD run evidence, logging/observability evidence.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Prepare the independently-built Meridian Stays capstone feature for a production-readiness demo with quality, security, deployment and observability evidence.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "Capstone"}'),
	(140, 5, 21, 'Day 9', 'Configuration Management, Scripting', '{"Day": "Day 9", "Week": "Week 2", "Topic": "Configuration Management, Scripting", "Module": "Automation & IaC", "Sub Topic": "Use Python or Shell for operational automation, parsing, health checks, configuration consistency and API-driven SRE tasks.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Operational Automation:\n• Write Python/Shell health-check automation for Order and Payment endpoints.\n• Parse logs to summarize error classes.\n• Validate configuration drift indicators.\n• Produce a machine-readable health report for CI/CD or runbook use.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4.6833", "Learning Outcomes": "Operational automation scripts + sample health report + log-parser output.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(171, 6, 21, NULL, 'Authentication and authorization at the gateway', '{"Topic": "Authentication and authorization at the gateway", "Sub-Category": "Azure API Management (APIM)", "Assessment Area": "Azure"}'),
	(172, 6, 22, NULL, 'Triggers and bindings (HTTP, Timer, Queue, Blob)', '{"Topic": "Triggers and bindings (HTTP, Timer, Queue, Blob)", "Sub-Category": "Azure Functions", "Assessment Area": "Azure"}'),
	(128, 4, 59, 'Day 38', 'Capstone Day 3 – Respond & Improve: Resilience & Edge-Case Hardening', '{"Day": "Day 38", "Week": "Week 8", "Topic": "Capstone Day 3 – Respond & Improve: Resilience & Edge-Case Hardening", "Module": "Capstone Project", "Sub Topic": "Simulate a backend failure/timeout on the booking-status journey end-to-end (detect, communicate, mitigate) on both React Web and React Native; expand automated test coverage for the newly hardened edge cases; add correlation-friendly logging so a user-visible error can be traced to the originating request; implement at least one reliability/UX improvement from the findings and update the implementation note.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Meridian Stays: Authenticated Booking Status & Stay Preferences – Resilience Hardening\nInput: The Day 24 feature and the Day 25 test/security/CI/CD hardening package.\nImplement: Simulate a backend failure/timeout on the booking-status journey, capture failure-path evidence, and implement at least one reliability/UX improvement across React Web and React Native.\nIntegrate: The failure simulation runs against the same feature and tests built independently in Days 24–25 — a review of the whole Meridian Stays feature as it now stands.\nValidate: Confirm the failure is detected, communicated and mitigated; confirm new tests fail pre-fix and pass post-fix.\nOutput: Simulated-failure evidence, expanded edge-case tests and a shipped reliability/UX improvement for the Meridian Stays feature.\nEvidence: Failure-path evidence, expanded tests, correlation-friendly logging output, the shipped improvement and updated implementation note.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Demonstrate resilient, cross-channel failure handling and evidence-based reliability improvement on the independently-built Meridian Stays capstone feature.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "Capstone"}'),
	(129, 4, 60, 'Day 39', 'Capstone Day 4 – Harden, Document & Demo', '{"Day": "Day 39", "Week": "Week 8", "Topic": "Capstone Day 4 – Harden, Document & Demo", "Module": "Capstone Project", "Sub Topic": "Finalize the CI/CD deployment path for the web/mobile deliverables; assemble the complete evidence pack (source code, test/CI evidence, security review, accessibility/performance findings, logging/observability evidence, acceptance-criteria traceability); rehearse and deliver the end-to-end capstone demonstration across backend contract, React Web and React Native.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Meridian Stays: Authenticated Booking Status & Stay Preferences – Harden, Document & Demo\nInput: All outputs from Days 24–26 on Meridian Stays.\nImplement: Finalize the CI/CD deployment path, assemble the full evidence pack, and rehearse/deliver the end-to-end capstone demonstration.\nIntegrate: The demonstration walks through the single, continuous Meridian Stays feature — Day 24''s journeys, Day 25''s quality/security evidence, Day 26''s resilience evidence — as one story.\nValidate: Confirm the deployment path runs end to end; confirm every acceptance criterion is traceable to evidence.\nOutput: Finalized CI/CD deployment path, complete production-readiness evidence pack, and rehearsed end-to-end demonstration for Meridian Stays.\nEvidence: Capstone repo/release, demo, tests, security review, CI/CD evidence, logging/observability evidence, all traced to acceptance criteria.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Deliver a production-readiness demo of an independently-built feature (Meridian Stays booking status & stay preferences) with quality, security, deployment, observability and acceptance-criteria evidence, ready for Milestone / SME review.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "Capstone"}'),
	(130, 5, 3, NULL, 'Pre-Assessment (Prerequisites)', '{"Week": "Pre-Program", "Topic": "Pre-Assessment (Prerequisites)", "Module": "Prerequisite Validation", "Sub Topic": "Baseline readiness check covering all prerequisite areas before the program begins: Linux & System Administration, AWS Fundamentals, Networking Fundamentals, CI/CD Basics, Monitoring & Alerting Basics, Incident Management Fundamentals, Scripting Basics (Python or Shell), Git & Infrastructure as Code Basics, Containers & Kubernetes Fundamentals, and Problem Solving & Debugging Skills. Delivered as a combination of Multiple-Choice Questions (MCQ) and a hands-on coding question. Learners who do not meet the baseline are directed to pre-work before Day 1.", "Hands-on Labs": "MCQ score report + coding-question submission and evaluation.", "Duration (Hrs)": "1.5", "Learning Outcomes": "Baseline readiness confirmed across all 10 prerequisite areas; gaps flagged for pre-work before Day 1."}'),
	(131, 5, 4, NULL, 'Trainer-Led Orientation', '{"Week": "Pre-Program", "Topic": "Trainer-Led Orientation", "Module": "Program Orientation", "Sub Topic": "Program kickoff led by the Trainer: introduce the RetailPulse 24x7 case study and the 90-plus session journey, walk through the curriculum structure, Milestone/Interview Bot/Capstone checkpoints, tooling and environment setup, and readiness/scoring criteria; address learner questions before Day 1 begins.", "Hands-on Labs": "Environment/tooling access confirmed + orientation attendance record.", "Duration (Hrs)": "2", "Learning Outcomes": "Learners understand the program structure, tooling access is verified, and expectations/readiness criteria are clear before Day 1."}'),
	(132, 5, 6, 'Day 1', 'SLI, SLO, SLA, Error Budgets', '{"Day": "Day 1", "Week": "Week 1", "Topic": "SLI, SLO, SLA, Error Budgets", "Module": "Reliability Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Establish service-level thinking; distinguish indicators, objectives and agreements; define measurable reliability targets and error-budget consumption for a production-like service.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Reliability Baseline:\n• Review RetailPulse 24x7 — E-Commerce Reliability Engineering Platform service map (Catalog, Order, Payment, Notification).\n• Select customer-critical Order API journey.\n• Define availability and latency SLIs.\n• Set initial SLO targets and error-budget calculation.\n• Record assumptions and measurement windows.", "Duration (Hrs)": "7", "Learning Outcomes": "SLO specification v1 + SLI definitions + error-budget worksheet committed to repository.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(133, 5, 7, 'Day 2', 'Availability, Resilience & Toil Reduction', '{"Day": "Day 2", "Week": "Week 1", "Topic": "Availability, Resilience & Toil Reduction", "Module": "Reliability Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Examine availability risks, resilience patterns, operational toil, manual work inventory and opportunities to reduce repetitive operations without weakening controls.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Reliability Risk & Toil Register:\n• Identify single points of failure in the Order journey.\n• Classify recurring operator tasks as toil/non-toil.\n• Prioritize 3 toil-reduction candidates.\n• Link resilience risks to the SLO/error budget from Session 1.", "Duration (Hrs)": "1", "Learning Outcomes": "Availability risk register + quantified toil backlog + remediation priorities.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(173, 6, 23, NULL, 'Hosting plans (Consumption, Premium, Dedicated)', '{"Topic": "Hosting plans (Consumption, Premium, Dedicated)", "Sub-Category": "Azure Functions", "Assessment Area": "Azure"}'),
	(174, 6, 24, NULL, 'Durable Functions and orchestration', '{"Topic": "Durable Functions and orchestration", "Sub-Category": "Azure Functions", "Assessment Area": "Azure"}'),
	(175, 6, 25, NULL, 'Scaling and monitoring', '{"Topic": "Scaling and monitoring", "Sub-Category": "Azure Functions", "Assessment Area": "Azure"}'),
	(141, 5, 22, 'Day 10', 'CI/CD, GitOps, Runbooks', '{"Day": "Day 10", "Week": "Week 2", "Topic": "CI/CD, GitOps, Runbooks", "Module": "Automation & IaC", "Sub Topic": "Integrate infrastructure/application changes with version control, deployment pipelines and operational runbooks; emphasize reviewable, repeatable change and rollback. Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, safety or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Lab 3 – Infrastructure as Code, Part D:\n• Store Terraform and service configuration in Git.\n• Add validation/deployment stages to the existing CI/CD flow.\n• Define GitOps-style change/approval path.\n• Create deploy, rollback and service-health runbooks.\n• Apply approved infrastructure change through pipeline.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "CI/CD evidence + Git-based change history + deployment/rollback runbooks + provisioned test environment.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(142, 5, 25, 'Day 11', 'Incident Response & Communication', '{"Day": "Day 11", "Week": "Week 3", "Topic": "Incident Response & Communication", "Module": "Incident & Problem Management", "Sub Topic": "Practice detection, acknowledgement, severity assessment, command structure, stakeholder communication, escalation and mitigation under time pressure. Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, safety or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Lab 5 – Incident Simulation, Part A:\n• Inject elevated Order API 5xx errors.\n• Detect via dashboard/alert.\n• Acknowledge and assign incident roles.\n• Build incident timeline.\n• Issue initial stakeholder communication.\n• Execute safe first mitigation.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Incident timeline + acknowledgement evidence + stakeholder update + mitigation record.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(143, 5, 26, 'Day 12', 'RCA, Postmortems, Problem Management', '{"Day": "Day 12", "Week": "Week 3", "Topic": "RCA, Postmortems, Problem Management", "Module": "Incident & Problem Management", "Sub Topic": "Move from incident symptom to contributing causes; distinguish root-cause analysis from blame; document corrective/preventive actions and problem ownership.", "Hands-on Labs": "Lab 5 – Incident Simulation, Part B:\n• Analyze telemetry from the injected failure.\n• Build causal chain and contributing-factor analysis.\n• Write blameless postmortem.\n• Create corrective actions with owners, priority and validation method.", "Duration (Hrs)": "6.1167", "Learning Outcomes": "Completed RCA + blameless postmortem + prioritized problem-management action register.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(144, 5, 27, 'Day 13', 'Runbooks, Playbooks', '{"Day": "Day 13", "Week": "Week 3", "Topic": "Runbooks, Playbooks", "Module": "Incident & Problem Management", "Sub Topic": "Create actionable operational procedures for known failure modes and decision-oriented response playbooks for uncertain incidents. Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, safety or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Lab 5 – Incident Simulation, Part C:\n• Convert RCA learning into a service runbook.\n• Create a high-error-rate response playbook with decision points.\n• Re-run the failure and compare response time/quality.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Validated runbook + incident playbook + repeat-simulation evidence.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(145, 5, 31, 'Day 14', 'Load Testing, Performance Engineering', '{"Day": "Day 14", "Week": "Week 3", "Topic": "Load Testing, Performance Engineering", "Module": "Performance & Capacity", "Sub Topic": "Design representative load, choose performance indicators, establish baselines, isolate bottlenecks and connect performance behavior to reliability objectives. Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, safety or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Lab 4 – Performance & Capacity, Part A:\n• Build k6/JMeter workload for browse → order → payment journey.\n• Run baseline, sustained and burst tests.\n• Capture latency, throughput, error rate and saturation.\n• Correlate bottlenecks with metrics/logs/traces.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Versioned load-test script + baseline performance report + identified bottleneck evidence.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(146, 5, 32, 'Day 15', 'Capacity Planning & Right-sizing', '{"Day": "Day 15", "Week": "Week 3", "Topic": "Capacity Planning & Right-sizing", "Module": "Performance & Capacity", "Sub Topic": "Translate observed utilization and demand into capacity requirements; evaluate headroom, scaling thresholds and right-sizing decisions.", "Hands-on Labs": "Lab 4 – Performance & Capacity, Part B:\n• Use load-test results to estimate required service capacity.\n• Calculate headroom for peak demand.\n• Review compute/database utilization.\n• Propose right-sized capacity and scaling thresholds.", "Duration (Hrs)": "2", "Learning Outcomes": "Capacity model + right-sizing recommendation + scaling-threshold specification.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(147, 5, 33, 'Day 16', 'Scalability & Cost Optimization (FinOps)', '{"Day": "Day 16", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "Scalability & Cost Optimization (FinOps)", "Module": "Performance & Capacity", "Sub Topic": "Balance reliability, scalability and cost; identify waste, test scaling behavior and document trade-offs without compromising SLOs.", "Hands-on Labs": "Lab 4 – Performance & Capacity, Part C:\n• Execute autoscaling test against peak profile.\n• Compare cost/capacity options against SLO impact.\n• Identify under/over-provisioned resources.\n• Produce reliability-aware optimization proposal.", "Duration (Hrs)": "2", "Learning Outcomes": "Performance baseline + capacity plan + scalability/FinOps recommendation + automation evidence.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(148, 5, 36, 'Day 17', 'HA/DR, Fault Tolerance', '{"Day": "Day 17", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "HA/DR, Fault Tolerance", "Module": "Resilience Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Design service continuity across component and zone failures; define redundancy, failover boundaries, recovery objectives and fault-tolerant behavior.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Resilience Design:\n• Identify HA requirements for ALB, service replicas and RDS.\n• Define fault-tolerance patterns for critical request path.\n• Set draft RTO/RPO for Order data.\n• Validate failure-domain coverage against architecture.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4.6667", "Learning Outcomes": "HA/DR design pack + RTO/RPO definitions + fault-tolerance checklist.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(176, 6, 26, NULL, 'Web App deployment and configuration', '{"Topic": "Web App deployment and configuration", "Sub-Category": "Azure App Services", "Assessment Area": "Azure"}'),
	(177, 6, 27, NULL, 'Deployment slots and swaps', '{"Topic": "Deployment slots and swaps", "Sub-Category": "Azure App Services", "Assessment Area": "Azure"}'),
	(150, 5, 38, 'Day 19', 'Backup, Restore & Disaster Recovery', '{"Day": "Day 19", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "Backup, Restore & Disaster Recovery", "Module": "Resilience Engineering", "Sub Topic": "Validate backup integrity, restoration steps and disaster-recovery procedures against recovery objectives rather than assuming recoverability. Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, safety or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – Recovery Drill:\n• Review backup policy for RDS/application data.\n• Execute restore into isolated test target.\n• Validate data/service recovery.\n• Compare achieved recovery time/point against RTO/RPO.\n• Update DR runbook.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Restore-test evidence + measured RTO/RPO result + updated disaster-recovery runbook.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(151, 5, 42, 'Day 20', 'AI for incident triage & alert correlation', '{"Day": "Day 20", "Week": "Week 4", "Topic": "AI for incident triage & alert correlation", "Module": "AI-Assisted SRE", "Sub Topic": "Use AI-assisted analysis to group related alerts, summarize incident context and accelerate triage while keeping human validation and operational controls.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – AI-Assisted Triage:\n• Feed a prepared multi-signal alert set from RetailPulse.\n• Correlate likely related alerts into one incident hypothesis.\n• Compare AI-assisted grouping with telemetry evidence.\n• Document confidence, false-correlation risks and human validation steps.", "Duration (Hrs)": "2", "Learning Outcomes": "AI-assisted triage report + correlated alert set + human-validation checklist.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(152, 5, 43, 'Day 21', 'Log analysis, RCA assistance', '{"Day": "Day 21", "Week": "Week 5", "Topic": "Log analysis, RCA assistance", "Module": "AI-Assisted SRE", "Sub Topic": "Apply AI assistance to large log sets and RCA hypothesis generation while verifying findings against metrics, traces, deployment history and known facts.", "Hands-on Labs": "Case Study – AI-Assisted RCA:\n• Analyze incident log bundle.\n• Generate candidate causal hypotheses.\n• Validate each hypothesis against traces/metrics/change history.\n• Produce evidence-backed RCA notes.", "Duration (Hrs)": "2", "Learning Outcomes": "Evidence-backed AI-assisted RCA notes + validation matrix.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "SPL"}'),
	(153, 5, 44, 'Day 22', 'Operational automation & chatops', '{"Day": "Day 22", "Week": "Week 5", "Topic": "Operational automation & chatops", "Module": "AI-Assisted SRE", "Sub Topic": "Build safe operational automation triggered through controlled commands/workflows, with auditability, guardrails and clear human approval boundaries. Delivered as an Instructor-Led (IL) session because of its complexity, safety or coordination needs; the mentor also opens with a guided review of the preceding Self-Paced (SPL) day(s) and resolves learner queries from those days before moving into today''s live topic.", "Hands-on Labs": "CAPSTONE – End-to-End Reliability Ownership:\n• Connect chatops/operational automation to approved health-check or mitigation workflow.\n• Demonstrate complete RetailPulse reliability lifecycle:\n  Build → Observe → Define SLOs → Automate → Test (Load/Failure) → Respond → RCA → Improve Reliability.\n• Reuse dashboards, Terraform, runbooks, performance evidence and resilience improvements produced earlier.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Capstone release + demo evidence + operational automation/chatops workflow.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "IL"}'),
	(154, 5, 49, 'Day 23', 'Capstone Project – PayStream247: A New, Independent Reliability Engagement', '{"Day": "Day 23", "Week": "Week 6", "Topic": "Capstone Project – PayStream247: A New, Independent Reliability Engagement", "Module": "Capstone Project", "Sub Topic": "Use Case: PayStream247 — a real-time payments and settlement platform (Authorization, Ledger/Settlement, Fraud-Detection, and Notification services) that the learner has NOT worked on during Days 1–22. This is a deliberately new system rather than a continuation of RetailPulse: instead of extending the running case study, the learner independently transfers every skill and artifact pattern built across Days 1–22 onto a fresh, unfamiliar production-like service — proving the ability to generalize, not replay memorized steps. Across these 4 consecutive project days the learner owns end-to-end reliability for the Authorize-to-Settle transaction journey: Build → Observe → Define SLOs → Automate → Test (Load/Failure) → Respond → RCA → Improve Reliability, including safe operational automation/chatops. Each day opens with a brief mentor checkpoint to review the previous day''s progress and unblock issues, then continues as self-directed capstone build time. Day-wise plan — Day 1: Build & Observe; Day 2: Automate & Test; Day 3: Respond, RCA & Improve; Day 4: Harden, Document & Demo — culminating in the full evidence pack and end-to-end demonstration assessed at Milestone 4.", "Hands-on Labs": "Capstone Day 1 – Build & Observe: Establish the PayStream247 Authorization-to-Settlement service baseline; instrument metrics/logs/traces and build dashboards; define SLIs/SLOs and error budgets across Authorization, Ledger/Settlement, Fraud-Detection and Notification services.", "Duration (Hrs)": "4", "Learning Outcomes": "Established service baseline + validated SLOs/telemetry evidence for the new, independent capstone platform.", "Learning Type (SPL / IL)": "Capstone"}'),
